<!--
    ServiceNow List Layout record-definition for the DEFAULT VIEW of the
    `x_casemgmt_case` table in the scoped application `x_casemgmt`.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
      AAP Section 0.4.4 requires the build to "configure form/list layouts
      on the three tables" and to "surface fields in the order shown in the
      data-model tables (subject, type, status, priority, description,
      requester_name, requester_email, opened_date, closed_date,
      assigned_group, assigned_agent)".

      Until this file was added, the package carried NO list-layout record
      at all, and the Default-view list layout that existed on the
      verification instance had been created outside the package. It held
      ten columns - number, assigned_agent, assigned_group, closed_date,
      description, opened_date, pending_reason, priority, requester_email,
      requester_name - i.e. everything EXCEPT the three columns an operator
      most needs: `status`, `subject` and `type`. A case list that shows a
      case number, a description and two empty assignment columns but not
      the case's subject or its status is not usable, and the omission was
      invisible to the package because no artifact described the layout.

      This record makes the layout part of the shipped application, in the
      AAP field order, with `number` first because it is the table's display
      value and therefore the column that carries the record link.

    COLUMN ORDER PRODUCED (position -> element)
        0  number             (auto-number, record link, CASE0000001 format)
        1  subject            AAP 0.4.4 order starts here
        2  type
        3  status
        4  priority
        5  description
        6  requester_name
        7  requester_email
        8  opened_date
        9  closed_date
       10  assigned_group
       11  assigned_agent
       12  pending_reason     (the app's own Pending-state reason field,
                               kept last; it was already present in the
                               pre-existing layout and is retained so the
                               Pending state remains readable from the list)

      `duration_to_close` (glide_duration) is intentionally NOT a column:
      it was only ever present in one administrator's PERSONALIZED list
      (see the note on sys_user below), never in the base layout, and it is
      not part of the AAP data model in Section 0.5.7.

    THE OTHER TWO TABLES NEED NO SUCH FILE
      Measured on the verification instance, the Default-view layouts for
      the sibling tables are already complete against their AAP field sets,
      so authoring records for them would change nothing:
        x_casemgmt_case_task  -> number, assigned_to, case, due_date,
                                 status, subject, type   (7 columns = all
                                 six AAP fields + number)
        x_casemgmt_case_party -> number, case, organization, party_type,
                                 person, role_label      (6 columns = all
                                 five AAP fields + number)
      Only the case table was missing columns, so only the case table gets
      a record here. This honours the AAP's MINIMAL CHANGE CLAUSE.

    SERIALIZATION SHAPE (why this file looks different from its siblings)
      A List Layout is not a plain record. `sys_ui_list_element` does NOT
      extend Application File (sys_metadata) - it has no `sys_scope` and no
      `sys_update_name` - so an element cannot be carried as an Update Set
      record of its own. The platform therefore serializes the whole layout
      as ONE update of type "List Layout": a `<record_update>` document
      whose root child is a `<sys_ui_list ... version="2">` CONTAINER that
      holds every `<sys_ui_list_element>` first and the actual
      `<sys_ui_list>` record last.

      This file reproduces that shape exactly as the platform emits it. It
      was copied from a live example on the verification instance
      (sys_update_xml `sys_ui_list_x_casemgmt_case_RPTee04fa982b136ad35c6fe1512826f6ab`,
      type "List Layout"), which is the platform's own output for this table,
      so the import path is the one the platform itself produces.

      Consequences worth knowing when reading this file:
        - The DEFAULT view is identified by an EMPTY view attribute on the
          container, while the record itself stores the literal string
          "Default view" in its view reference column and every element
          carries view="Default view" inside its list_id display hint. That
          asymmetry is the platform's own convention, not a typo: it was
          verified against the platform-authored payloads
          sys_ui_list_sys_hub_flow_null and sys_ui_list_sys_atf_test_suite_null
          held in sys_update_version on the verification instance, and
          against the out-of-box base layouts for the problem and
          change_request tables, all of which store "Default view" in that
          reference column even though no sys_ui_view row of that name
          exists anywhere on the instance. A NAMED view instead carries the
          view's name in the container attribute and the view's sys_id in
          the record.
        - Each element's `<list_id>` points at the layout record defined at
          the bottom of this same document, so the document is
          self-contained: nothing outside this payload has to exist first.
        - `<sys_update_name>sys_ui_list_x_casemgmt_case_null</sys_update_name>`
          is the platform's own naming for a layout on a null (default)
          view, and it is the key the import engine matches on. That is what
          makes this record UPDATE an existing default-view layout on the
          target instance rather than adding a second one.

    THE `sys_user` FIELD MUST STAY EMPTY - AND WHY IT MATTERS
      `sys_ui_list.sys_user` distinguishes a BASE layout (empty - applies to
      everyone) from a PERSONALIZED layout (a user's own column choice,
      which silently overrides the base layout for that user only).

      On the verification instance there were TWO Default-view layout rows
      for `x_casemgmt_case`: the base one, and a personalized one belonging
      to the administrator (sys_user populated, eleven columns) that had
      been created by clicking the list's column-picker. While a
      personalized row exists, the list toolbar reads "Update Personalized
      List" and the base layout is NOT what that user sees - so a fix to the
      base layout alone would look like it had no effect.

      This record is the BASE layout: `<sys_user/>` is empty. A verifier who
      finds a personalized Default-view row for this table should delete it
      so that the shipped layout is the one in force. Personalized rows are
      per-user state, are not carried by an Update Set, and are never part
      of this application.

    NO DATA IS AFFECTED
      A list layout only decides which columns render. It creates no
      records, changes no field values, and enforces no security: column
      visibility is still subject to the field-level ACLs in ../acl/.

    IDENTIFIERS
      List record sys_id 35145a169322cb10830ef82bdd03d6e7 is pinned to the
      Default-view layout row that already exists on the verification
      instance, so this record updates that row instead of racing it. The
      import engine matches on `sys_update_name` first (see above), so the
      pinned value is a convenience, not a dependency.

      Every element sys_id is a recomputable MD5 of a canonical string,
      `md5("x_casemgmt_case.list.default." + element)`:
        number           b902b461a7d845af3a7855ef54517782
        subject          3e23d89436716d4009cd8b28bbc276ed
        type             04a55bfb47d0896e92ed9064bea4f167
        status           3465363256d9f1c2464c8db6741c0bec
        priority         8541f38dd188ff731dacc94054eeaea8
        description      f3a5942f6916ddf2143095d296b401c6
        requester_name   3e84f9082bcd5ad3289d6251f1eeb3a1
        requester_email  5fae7d329ff3285af41106995327159a
        opened_date      e2ce9e08a0e6445dabd7a86538c1eaf0
        closed_date      b802a30d601e89f561e8c00c846ab532
        assigned_group   c90e9971cdc99482c6db90b7a91fe83c
        assigned_agent   af1e7c283ec90b7f2e546b7caa660e65
        pending_reason   17b3bd08c5d712240692208a65c328dc
      Recomputable via:
        printf '%s' 'x_casemgmt_case.list.default.status' | md5sum | cut -c1-32
      so regenerating this file is byte-deterministic.

    CROSS-REFERENCES (AAP Section 0.5.2 dependency ordering)
      MUST LOAD AFTER:
        - ../app/sys_app/x_casemgmt_case_management.xml (scope authority)
        - ../tables/x_casemgmt_case.xml (the table being listed)
        - ../dictionary/x_casemgmt_case_*.xml (every column named below
          must exist as a dictionary entry before it can be a list column)
      MUST LOAD BEFORE:
        - ../seed-data/cases/x_casemgmt_case_demo_*.xml (so the demo cases
          are readable in the list immediately after commit)

    AAP COMPLIANCE
      - AAP 0.4.4 list layout configured on the case table, in the AAP
        field order, with status/subject/type present.
      - AAP 0.7.2 scoped-namespace exclusivity: the record carries the
        scoped application's sys_scope/sys_package; no global list layout
        is touched.
      - AAP 0.7.2 minimal change: only the one table whose layout was
        deficient is described here.
      - No PII: column names only.
-->
